C++ Area Calculator Using Function Overloading
Description

This C++ program demonstrates function overloading by calculating the areas of different shapes using functions with the same name, calculateArea().

The calculateArea() functions have different parameter lists, so the compiler selects the appropriate function according to the arguments provided.

Functions Used
1. calculateArea(int side) Function

This function calculates the area of a square.

side stores the side length of the square.
It returns side × side.
It is called when one integer value is passed.
2. calculateArea(int length, int width) Function

This function calculates the area of a rectangle.

length stores the length of the rectangle.
width stores the width of the rectangle.
It returns length × width.
It is called when two integer values are passed.
3. calculateArea(double radius) Function

This function calculates the area of a circle.

radius stores the radius of the circle.
It uses the value of π (PI).
It returns π × radius × radius.
It is called when one double value is passed.
Main Function

In the main() function, the overloaded calculateArea() functions are called for:

Square with side 5
Rectangle with length 6 and width 4
Circle with radius 2.0

The calculated areas are then displayed.

Output
Square Area: 25
Rectangle Area: 24
Circle Area: 12.5664
OOP Concept Used

Compile-Time Polymorphism — Function Overloading

             calculateArea()
              /     |      \
             /      |       \
        Square   Rectangle   Circle
       1 int     2 int       1 double

The same function name calculateArea() performs different operations depending on the parameters passed. This demonstrates function overloading.